<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saulteaux</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Plains Ojibwe, Nakawē</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Saulteaux are a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ojibwe">Ojibwe</a> people, named by the French after the rapids of Sault Ste. Marie. In the 18th and 19th centuries many moved west into present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saskatchewan">Saskatchewan</a>, adopting horses and bison hunting as the Plains Ojibwe and allying with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cree">Cree</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assiniboine">Assiniboine</a>. Many Saulteaux First Nations signed the numbered treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saulteaux.jpg" alt="Saulteaux" width="320" height="311" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DarrenBaker · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anishinaabe-Anishinini_Distribution_Map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Saulteaux are also called the Anihšināpē (Anishinaabe), and their name is written in several forms, among them Salteaux and Saulteau. The French word means people of the rapids or waterfalls and refers to their former home around Sault Ste. Marie, on the St. Marys River between Lake Superior and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-huron">Lake Huron</a>. They lived chiefly by hunting and fishing and traded widely with the French, the British and later the Americans at that post. Their historic lands lay around Lake Superior and Lake Winnipeg, chiefly about Sault Ste. Marie and northern Michigan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Pressure from European Canadians and Americans gradually pushed them west. In the late 18th and early 19th centuries, as partners of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cree">Cree</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>, Saulteaux groups moved north-west into the Swan River and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumberland">Cumberland</a> districts of west-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a>, and into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saskatchewan">Saskatchewan</a> along the Assiniboine River as far as its confluence with the Souris River. There they took on cultural traits of their allies, the Plains Cree and Assiniboine, forming a mixed culture of woodland and plains customs. Because they were moved to land ill-suited to European crops, they kept much of the territory assigned to them as reserves. Among the numbered treaties, Saulteaux First Nations signed Treaty 1, Treaty 2, Treaty 3, Treaty 4, Treaty 6 and Treaty 8.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Saulteaux have three main divisions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> or Eastern Saulteaux live around Rainy Lake and Lake of the Woods, speak a form of Anishinaabemowin sometimes called the Northwestern Ojibwa language, and keep a culture descended from that of the Eastern Woodlands. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a> or Central Saulteaux, in eastern and southern Manitoba, have a culture between woodland and plains; they and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> population have been called Bungi, from bangii, a little bit.</p><p>The Western Saulteaux, centred in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saskatchewan">Saskatchewan</a> and reaching into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberta">Alberta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a>, call themselves Nakawē, while the Plains Cree call them Nahkawiyiniw. Their language, Nakawēmowin or Western Ojibwa, is Algonquian, and their culture is that of the Plains. Together the Manitoba and Western Saulteaux are sometimes called the Plains Ojibwe. Today English is the first language of many Saulteaux. Their communities include Cote First Nation at Kamsack, Peguis in Manitoba and the Saulteau First Nations at Moberly Lake.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saulteaux” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>As they moved west onto the plains and took up horses and bison hunting, <b>Saulteaux</b> and later Métis groups joined them in an alliance the Cree call Nehiyaw-Pwat.</blockquote><a href="/state/iron-confederacy">Iron Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
